<Annex
  title="The Fine-Tuning Problem"
  piece="04"
  eyebrow="Builder's Annex · 04 · Fine-tuning"
  dek="Fine-tuning an MLIP wears the clothes of an ordinary fine-tune. Three physical facts break the analogy, and the tooling that handles them is about two years old."
  prevHref="/annex/the-performance"
  prevLabel="Where the Time Goes"
  nextHref="/annex/the-callers"
  nextLabel="The Callers"
>
  <p>
    A user shows up with a foundation checkpoint and a folder of DFT snapshots. The checkpoint is
    UMA-medium or MACE-MP-0, a general-purpose potential trained on most of the periodic table. The
    folder holds a few hundred to a few thousand labeled structures of one system they actually care
    about: a quaternary alloy, an aluminosilicate zeolite, a supported catalyst under reaction
    conditions. The foundation model is decent on their system and not good enough. They want good
    enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    The mechanics look like any fine-tune you have shipped. Load the pretrained weights, pick a small
    learning rate, run gradient descent on a loss that compares predicted energies and forces to the
    DFT labels, freeze the early layers or don't. If you have fine-tuned a vision backbone or a
    language model, the muscle memory transfers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three physical facts break the analogy. The labels are not what they appear to be. The force loss
    makes the autograd graph second-order, which collides with the exact kernels that make these
    models fast to serve. And the thing you optimize, held-out error on snapshots, is not the thing
    you need, which is a stable trajectory. Each has grown its own small body of tooling over the
    last two years, some of it good, most of it young.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem 1: the labels sit on a hidden baseline</h2>

  <p>
    A DFT total energy is a big number. A modest supercell of a transition-metal oxide might come
    back from the DFT code at roughly &minus;1000 eV. Almost none of that number is chemistry. It is
    the sum of per-element isolated-atom energies: the energy each atom would have sitting alone in a
    vacuum, which for a heavy element is hundreds of eV of core and valence structure that never
    changes when you move the atom around inside a solid. The part that responds to geometry, the
    part a potential exists to predict, is the remainder after you subtract that baseline. For a
    &minus;1000 eV cell, the baseline is around &minus;995 eV and the binding remainder is around
    &minus;5 eV.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every MLIP handles this by subtracting a fixed per-element reference before training. You pass in
    a table, one energy per element, and the model only ever sees and predicts the small remainder.
    This is load-bearing, not a preprocessing nicety: made to regress the raw &minus;1000 eV
    directly, the 0.5% of the signal that carries the physics would drown under the 99.5% that is
    constant per composition.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the trap. Those per-element references are not universal constants. They are outputs of a
    specific DFT recipe: the exchange-correlation functional (PBE, PBEsol, r²SCAN), the
    pseudopotentials, the plane-wave cutoff, the code and its defaults. Change the recipe and every
    isolated-atom energy shifts. The foundation model baked in the references from its pretraining
    recipe. If your user computed their 800 snapshots with a different functional, or the same
    functional in a different code with different pseudopotentials, every one of their total energies
    is offset from the model's expectation by a constant that depends only on composition.
  </p>

  <p>
    The offset is per-element and rigid. A structure with 12 aluminum and 24 oxygen atoms is wrong by
    12 times the aluminum reference error plus 24 times the oxygen reference error, exactly, for
    every such structure. Forces are blind to it: a force is the negative gradient of energy with
    respect to position, the gradient of a constant is zero, so a per-composition energy offset
    contributes nothing to any force. Your force MAE comes back beautiful. Your energy error on any
    single fixed composition also looks fine once the model absorbs the offset into its bias. The
    failure surfaces only when you compare energies across compositions: formation energies, defect
    energies, adsorption energies, anything that subtracts the energy of one composition from
    another. Those are silently, systematically wrong, and they are usually the exact quantities the
    user is fine-tuning to get.
  </p>

