  <p>
    The fix is cheap and belongs in the tooling, not in a documentation note the user will skip.
    Before training, refit the per-element references to the user's data by linear least-squares:
    build the matrix of element counts per structure, solve for the per-element energies that best
    explain the user's total energies, use those as the new references. It is a linear regression
    with as many unknowns as distinct elements, solvable in milliseconds. Do it and the offset
    vanishes into the references where it belongs; skip it and you ship a model that reports perfect
    forces and lies about every phase boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem 2: force training is second-order autograd</h2>

  <p>
    The force is already a derivative. The model computes an energy, and autograd differentiates it
    with respect to the input positions to get forces, inside the forward pass a user never sees. Put
    a loss on those forces and backpropagate it to the weights and you are differentiating a quantity
    that is itself a gradient. The backward pass traverses the graph a second time. This is
    double-backward, and PyTorch supports it, but it costs: roughly 2 to 3 times the memory and
    wall-clock of an energy-only step, because the intermediate activations of the first derivative
    all have to be retained and differentiated again. The blow-up is on activations, not weights, so
    it scales with batch size and cutoff radius rather than model size. You manage it by shrinking the
    batch.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sharper problem is that double-backward fights the fast path. The kernels that make these
    models fast, NVIDIA's cuEquivariance fused equivariant operations plus <code>torch.compile</code>
    and CUDA-graph capture, are built and tested for the forward pass and the first backward, the
    operations you run when serving. They do not currently support double-backward. The relevant
    cuEquivariance issue, #268, is exactly this. So a force-label fine-tune either drops those kernels
    or runs them eager, and your fine-tuning code path diverges from your serving code path right
    here. The card you serve on, tuned for compiled throughput, is not the card you want for
    fine-tuning, which needs headroom for the doubled activation memory and eager execution.
    Fine-tuning wants the bigger GPU and a different software stack on it. Budget for two tiers.
  </p>

  <p>
    A related wrinkle sits on the model side. Some potentials are conservative: forces are the true
    gradient of the energy, which is what makes double-backward necessary and what guarantees energy
    conservation in MD. Others predict forces from a separate head, directly, dodging the second
    derivative at inference. Orb-v3 ships both variants. The released UMA and eSEN checkpoints are
    conservative-only, trained with a direct head as a scaffold that was removed before release. If a
    user's checkpoint is conservative, force fine-tuning is double-backward, with no eager-versus-fused
    choice to make about it.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem 3: "did it work" is dynamical, not a static number</h2>

  <p>
    Low energy and force error on held-out snapshots is necessary. It is not sufficient, and the gap
    between the two is where most fine-tuned potentials fail in production.
  </p>

  <p>
    A static test set samples the configurations the DFT happened to compute. A molecular dynamics
    trajectory samples the configurations the potential's own energy landscape leads it into, which
    include rare, high-force, near-pathological geometries a held-out set of relaxed or lightly
    perturbed structures barely touches. A model can have 20 meV/Å force MAE on the test set and
    still place a spurious low-energy basin somewhere the test set never looked. In MD the trajectory
    finds that basin. The solid melts at 300 K, or the energy drifts upward over 100 ps until the run
    explodes, or a bond quietly dissociates that should never break. Randomly distributed pointwise
    errors average out; errors that are systematically wrong in the configurations a trajectory
    dwells in do not, and static metrics cannot tell you which kind you have.
  </p>

