  </figure>

  <p>
    So the acceptance test is a simulation, not a scalar. Run MD from the fine-tuned model. Confirm
    it is stable: no explosion, no monotonic energy drift in a constant-energy run over a meaningful
    horizon. Then confirm it reproduces a number the user already trusts from experiment or the
    reference method: a lattice constant, a bulk density, a radial-distribution-function peak, a
    melting-point bracket. That reproduction is the artifact that certifies the fine-tune. A loss
    curve certifies that optimization happened. I would not accept a fine-tuned potential into
    anything downstream without the MD report.
  </p>

  <h2>Catastrophic forgetting and replay</h2>

  <p>
    Fine-tune a general potential hard enough on 500 snapshots of one zeolite and it gets very good
    at that zeolite and measurably worse at everything else. The weights that encoded broad chemistry
    drift toward the narrow distribution you are hammering them with. This is ordinary catastrophic
    forgetting, and it bites here because the broad chemistry you are eroding is the entire reason you
    started from a foundation model.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix is replay: mix a slice of the original pretraining distribution back into the fine-tuning
    batches so the optimizer keeps paying rent on general chemistry while it specializes. MACE's
    fine-tuning path does this by default, replaying a slice of its MPtrj pretraining set alongside
    the user's structures. The mixing ratio is a knob, and defaulting it on is the right call,
    because a user fine-tuning on one zeolite is not thinking about the silicon chemistry they are
    about to forget.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the field is developing</h2>

  <p>
    The foundation-model-plus-fine-tune paradigm for interatomic potentials is about two years old.
    Before it, you trained a potential from scratch on your system, every time. UMA was trained
    across five FAIR chemistry datasets totaling roughly 500M structures; MACE-MP-0 was released as a
    pretrained base to build on. The user no longer buys the whole model. They buy the last mile, a
    few hundred structures of specialization on top of a backbone that already knows what a metal is.
    That shift is what makes any of this a fine-tuning problem at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    The multi-recipe question, several DFT recipes served from one backbone, has one interesting
    production answer so far. UMA-medium is 1.4B total parameters but activates only about 50M per
    structure through a Mixture of Linear Experts. A lightweight router reads the structure's dataset,
    task, total charge, and spin and produces mixing weights over a set of expert weight matrices.
    The experts are linear, so the router collapses them into a single effective weight matrix before
    the forward pass runs, and the forward is then a normal dense model at ~50M active parameters with
    no per-layer routing overhead. For infrastructure that has to serve users on different DFT recipes
    from shared weights, that is the shape of the answer: condition the backbone on the recipe rather
    than fork a checkpoint per recipe.
  </p>

  <p>
    LoRA is conspicuously rare in this corner, and the reason is diagnostic. LoRA earns its keep when
    parameter memory is the binding constraint, when the base is tens of billions of parameters and
    full-rank updates are unaffordable to store or optimize. These models are 5M to 1.4B parameters
    and the active forward is small. Parameter memory is not what hurts. Second-order autograd is, and
    it hurts on the activation graph, not the weight count. A LoRA adapter on the linear and
    tensor-product weights would reduce the number of trained parameters and do nothing about the
    double-backward cost that dominates force-label fine-tuning. You would carry LoRA's complexity and
    keep the bill you were trying to cut. Until someone finds an adapter that shrinks the
    second-derivative graph rather than the parameter set, low-rank tricks are solving the problem
    these models do not have.
  </p>

  <h2>What I would build</h2>

  <p>
    Fine-tune the smallest foundation checkpoint that passes the dynamical acceptance test. A larger
    backbone buys extra static accuracy that reliably fails to survive contact with MD, and it costs
    you double-backward memory the whole way. Start small, escalate only when the MD report demands
    it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Automate the three things users get wrong on their own: refit the per-element references by
    least-squares before every run, check recipe consistency and warn loudly on a detected offset,
    turn replay on by default with a sane mixing ratio. These are not user decisions. They are
    correctness invariants, and every one of them is invisible in the loss curve.
  </p>

  <p>
    And make the acceptance artifact an MD run. A fine-tune that ships with a stable 200 ps trajectory
    and a reproduced lattice constant is a fine-tune. A fine-tune that ships with a loss curve is a
    hope.
  </p>
</Annex>
